✅ What is ACID in Databases?
ACID is an acronym that stands for:

A: Atomicity

C: Consistency

I: Isolation

D: Durability

These are the key properties that ensure reliable processing of database transactions.

🔹 1. Atomicity
Atomicity means that a transaction is treated as a single unit.
→ Either all the operations within the transaction happen, or none of them do.

📌 Example: If you transfer money from Account A to Account B, both the debit and credit operations must succeed. If one fails, the entire transaction is rolled back.

🔹 2. Consistency
Consistency ensures that the database always stays in a valid state.
→ Any transaction will take the database from one valid state to another.

📌 Example: If a table has a rule that "balance cannot be negative," the transaction must not violate that rule.

🔹 3. Isolation
Isolation means that the operations of one transaction are invisible to other transactions until they are complete.
→ Multiple transactions can occur at the same time without interfering with each other.

📌 Example: If two users are booking the same seat at the same time, isolation ensures only one succeeds.

🔹 4. Durability
Durability guarantees that once a transaction is committed, it will remain so—even in the case of a power failure, crash, or error.
→ Committed data is permanently saved.

📌 Example: After your bank transfer is completed, it will not be lost even if the server crashes right afterward.


✅ Summary Table:
| Property    | What it Ensures                                     |
| ----------- | --------------------------------------------------- |
| Atomicity   | All or nothing execution of a transaction           |
| Consistency | Data remains valid before and after the transaction |
| Isolation   | Transactions do not interfere with each other       |
| Durability  | Committed data is permanently stored                |
